<p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
By <a href="/en/urban-gavelin/" rel="author">Urban Gavelin</a>, <time datetime="2026-11-02">November 2, 2026</time>, Sales Psychology &amp; Objection Handling
</p>
<h1>When the Customer Says She Needs to Think About It</h1>
<img src={coverImage} alt="When the Customer Says She Needs to Think About It" width="1280" height="720" class="hero-img" />
<p class="lead"><strong>We need to think about it. Every salesperson has heard the phrase, and most have learned a response to it. I had a rule for it for many years. A few years ago I broke my own rule, and it cost me a deal that didn't need to be lost.</strong></p>

<div class="section">
<h2>The Rule I Broke</h2>
<p>The rule was simple. When a customer says she needs to think about it, I don't treat it as an argument to counter. I treat it as a sign that I haven't explained something clearly enough. Hesitation is rarely about needing time. It's usually something the customer doesn't dare say, or doesn't have the words for.</p><p>That's why I probe. I want to understand what's really behind the hesitation before I respond to it.</p><p>The meeting was with a company that sells medical technology equipment. The sales director was interested, the questions were good, and everything pointed in the right direction. Near the end, hesitation appeared. It wasn't large, but it was clear.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>What Hesitation Really Means</h2>
<p>That time, I didn't ask. I let it go, said we'd be in touch, and left with a feeling that it would probably work itself out. It didn't. The deal never happened, and I still don't know why. That's what makes it costly. I didn't lose to a counterargument I couldn't handle. I lost to a question I never asked.</p><p>Hesitation in a buying decision is almost always about fear, and fear isn't something people say out loud easily. The fear of choosing the wrong supplier and having to answer for it. The fear of not getting the rest of leadership on board. The fear of taking on one more project nobody has time for. None of these sound good to say to a salesperson, so they get dressed up as "we need to think about it."</p><p>If the salesperson responds with a counterargument, it gets worse. The customer now has to defend an objection she doesn't actually hold. If the salesperson lets it go, as I did, the fear gets to grow undisturbed once you've parted ways.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>The Seller's Own Fear</h2>
<p>There's a fear on the seller's side too, and it's easier to miss. Asking what the hesitation is really about can feel intrusive. The meeting has gone well, the mood is good, and the question risks ruining it. So you let it go and call it respect for the customer's need to think things over.</p><p>That's usually not respect. It's comfort, and it's the customer who pays for it when she's left alone with her hesitation. What works is taking the responsibility yourself. Say that you probably haven't explained something well enough, and ask what needs to be clearer. That makes it easier for the customer to tell the truth, because you've already taken the blame.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Do This This Week</h2>
<ul><li>Next time someone says they need to think about it, don't respond with an argument. Say you may not have explained something clearly enough, and ask what's unclear.</li><li>Ask for the thing that's hard to say: "What would need to be true for this to feel safe?"</li><li>Bring up the most common concerns yourself, early in the meeting, before the customer has to.</li><li>Review the last three deals that needed to "think about it." Do you know why they hesitated? If not, reach out and ask.</li><li>Never leave a meeting with just "we'll be in touch." Agree together on what happens next and when.</li></ul><p>Which of your recent "we need to think about it" moments was really something the customer didn't dare say out loud?</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">What does 'I need to think about it' usually mean?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">It's rarely about actual thinking time. More often it signals an unspoken fear, such as worry about choosing the wrong supplier or failing to get leadership buy-in, that the customer doesn't feel comfortable voicing directly.</p>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">How do you ask about hesitation without sounding pushy?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">Take the responsibility yourself first by saying you may not have explained something clearly enough, then ask what needs clarifying. This removes the pressure from the customer to defend an objection and makes it easier for her to share the real concern.</p>
</div>
</div>

